86Box's FIFO thread yields the command ring to its own FIFO, so the guest's
one order is kept (doc 21 §13, M14).

The guest has one bus into the chip: a write it puts in the memory FIFO
(an LFB or texture write, `voodoo->fifo`) before a word it puts in the
command ring has to reach the pixel pipeline first. `voodoo_fifo_thread`
drained the memory FIFO once per wake and then stayed in the ring for as
long as the guest kept feeding it -- which in a game is the whole frame,
measured ~19,000 words behind all race long -- so an LFB write waited
there while the swap that followed it in the ring was consumed, and landed
in the buffer that swap had just turned into the back one. Carmageddon's
HUD flashed in and out for exactly this (2026-09-18, the user's run).

The ring loop now yields the moment anything appears in the memory FIFO,
and the whole pass repeats, so the two are interleaved in the guest's own
order. Three lines: a `do`, `&& FIFO_EMPTY` in the ring loop's condition,
and a `while (!FIFO_EMPTY)` at the end; the loop bodies are left at their
own indentation so the diff stays what it is.

It costs nothing -- no wait anywhere, the same work in a different order --
and it is what a chip with one FIFO does. The one case it cannot reach is a
consumer already parked inside `cmdfifo_get` waiting for the rest of a
packet the guest has not finished writing; `voodoo/voodoo2.c` counts those
(`N publishes behind the LFB queue` in the 5 s line) and they are 0 in
every run so far.

The files are 86Box's, vendored verbatim in voodoo/86box and overlaid into
hw/voodoo by prepare-qemu.sh, which is why this is a patch on the overlay
rather than an edit there. Drop it when upstream 86Box has the fix and
scripts/sync-86box-voodoo.sh brings it in.

diff --git a/hw/voodoo/86box/vid_voodoo_fifo.c b/hw/voodoo/86box/vid_voodoo_fifo.c
index 720b8f8..6b16fe0 100644
--- a/hw/voodoo/86box/vid_voodoo_fifo.c
+++ b/hw/voodoo/86box/vid_voodoo_fifo.c
@@ -388,6 +388,16 @@ voodoo_fifo_thread(void *param)
         thread_wait_event(voodoo->wake_fifo_thread, -1);
         thread_reset_event(voodoo->wake_fifo_thread);
         voodoo->voodoo_busy = 1;
+        /* One order (2ksbox): the guest has one bus into the chip, so a
+           write it put in this FIFO before a word it put in the command
+           ring has to run before that word. Draining this FIFO once and
+           then staying in the ring for as long as the guest keeps feeding
+           it -- which in a game is the whole frame -- breaks that: a HUD
+           written through the LFB waits here while the swap that follows
+           it in the ring is consumed, and lands in the buffer the swap has
+           just turned into the back one. So the ring loop below yields the
+           moment anything appears here, and this pass runs again. */
+        do {
         while (!FIFO_EMPTY) {
             uint64_t      start_time = plat_timer_read();
             uint64_t      end_time;
@@ -480,7 +490,7 @@ voodoo_fifo_thread(void *param)
         thread_set_event(voodoo->fifo_empty_event);
         ATOMIC_STORE(voodoo->fifo_empty_signaled, 1);
 
-        while (voodoo->cmdfifo_enabled && (voodoo->cmdfifo_depth_rd != voodoo->cmdfifo_depth_wr || voodoo->cmdfifo_in_sub)) {
+        while (voodoo->cmdfifo_enabled && FIFO_EMPTY && (voodoo->cmdfifo_depth_rd != voodoo->cmdfifo_depth_wr || voodoo->cmdfifo_in_sub)) {
             uint64_t start_time = plat_timer_read();
             uint64_t end_time;
             uint32_t header = cmdfifo_get(voodoo);
@@ -1072,6 +1082,7 @@ voodoo_fifo_thread(void *param)
             end_time = plat_timer_read();
             voodoo->time += end_time - start_time;
         }
+        } while (!FIFO_EMPTY);          /* the ring loop yielded: drain again */
 
         voodoo->voodoo_busy = 0;
     }
